ld only run if `epel_next` is true. So I\'ll use a conditional block with \'when: epel_next\' and notify the handler again.\n\nI also need to structure these tasks correctly in YAML. Each task should be a list under the main tasks key. I\'ll use the \'name\' field for each task\'s description, and include the necessary modules and parameters.\n\nI should avoid adding any explanations or comments beyond what\'s necessary for the YAML structure. Also, ensure that the playbook starts with \'---\' followed by a line break before the tasks begin.\n\nLet me think about possible issues. For Task 1, I need to make sure that the condition correctly filters the systems. Using ansible_os_family == "RedHat" should cover both RHEL and Amazon Linux. Then, checking the major version with redhat_version | int >=7 and <=10 seems correct.\n\nFor the EPEL tasks, I need to use the variables as provided. I\'ll make sure that the states are set to present to ensure the packages are installed.\n\nI also need to remember to include the notify: Update package cache in both Task 3 and Task 4 so that the package cache is updated after installations.\n\nPutting it all together, I\'ll structure each task with the correct module, parameters, and conditions. I\'ll make sure the YAML syntax is correct, with proper indentation and no trailing spaces.\n\nI think I\'ve covered all the requirements. Now, I\'ll write out the YAML playbook accordingly.\n</think>\n\n```yaml\n---\n- name: Check OS compatibility\n  ansible.builtin.meta:\n    clear_facts: no\n  when: ansible_os_family == "RedHat" and (ansible_distribution == "Amazon" or (ansible_distribution == "RedHat" and (ansible_redhat_version | int >=7 and ansible_redhat_version | int <=10)))\n  run_once: true\n\n- name: Install epel gpg key\n  ansible yum:\n    key: "{{ epel_gpg_key }}"\n    state: present\n\n- name: Install epel-release package\n  ansible yum:\n    name: "{{ epel_url }}"\n    state: present\n  notify: Update package cache\n\n- name: 